You're misunderstanding or misrepresenting what was said.  The code may 
be written logically in the language.  However, the way it behaves may 
not be logical to us, because to use, logically, the messages should flow 
in the order they happen in the context of the game -- dart hits, poison 
wears off.  The code may not care, because it doesn't have the contextual 
understanding of the story being told by the game.

I write code all the time, mostly for work, and within the constraints of 
the language it's written in (be it Perl, C, or Java), it can be 
perfectly logical.  But the output may not make logical sense to me, in 
the greater sense.  The language just follows the procedures it's given.  
That doesn't mean it makes sense in the way we expect it to.  

He never said the code was written illogically.  He said it behaved 
illogically.  These are two entirely different things, as evidenced by 
this ordering mishap.  


